---
// The four Headline findings, one chapter each. A chart ticket fills a chapter by adding
// <TheChart slot="chart" /> inside it; see site/README.md.
import Chapter from './Chapter.astro';
---

<div class="wrap">
  <Chapter
    number={1}
    title="Displacement tripled"
    chartTitle="Displacement rose every year; Net change moved further below zero"
    chartSubtitle="Share of workforce, quarterly average across 80 countries"
    chartRatio="500 / 285"
    chartPlaceholder="Line chart: Displacement and Net change by quarter"
  >
    <p>
      Across 80 countries, average Displacement rose from 2.7% in 2020 to 8.7% in the latest four
      quarters.
    </p>
    <p>New roles did not keep pace: Net change fell from −0.9% to −3.3%.</p>
    <p slot="definition">
      <b>Displacement</b> is the gross share of a Sector's workforce whose roles were lost to AI in a
      quarter. <b>Net change</b> offsets it with the new roles AI created; negative means more lost than
      created.
    </p>
    <Fragment slot="source">Shaded bands mark the two periods compared.</Fragment>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter
    number={2}
    title="The Sector gap"
    chartTitle="Displacement by Sector"
    chartSubtitle="Share of workforce, average of the latest four quarters"
    chartRatio="500 / 284"
    chartPlaceholder="Ranked bars: Displacement by Sector"
  >
    <p>Administrative & Clerical has the highest Displacement of the ten Sectors, at 14.2%.</p>
    <p>
      Healthcare & Life Sciences (3.3%) and Education & Research (3.9%) sit at the other end, at less
      than a third of that.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter
    number={3}
    title="Rich countries lose more but recover more"
    chartTitle="Displacement and Replacement rate both rise with income"
    chartSubtitle="By World Bank income group, average of the latest four quarters"
    chartRatio="500 / 174"
    chartPlaceholder="Paired bars: Displacement and Replacement rate by income group"
  >
    <p>
      High-income countries show the highest Displacement, 13.1%, against 4.7% in low-income countries.
    </p>
    <p>
      They also show the highest Replacement rate: 75 new roles for every 100 displaced, against 20.
    </p>
    <p slot="definition">
      <b>Replacement rate</b> is the number of new roles AI created for every 100 roles displaced.
    </p>
  </Chapter>

  <Chapter
    number={4}
    title="Women in clerical work"
    chartTitle="One Sector stands apart"
    chartSubtitle="Women's share of the workforce and of displaced roles, by Sector"
    chartRatio="500 / 272"
    chartPlaceholder="Dot pairs: women's share of workforce and of displaced roles"
  >
    <p>Women are 79% of the Administrative & Clerical workforce but 95% of its displaced roles.</p>
    <p>In every other Sector the two shares are within about 4 points of each other.</p>
  </Chapter>
</div>
